"""Phase 2.1 NEXUS knowledge, bank B: PLANNED items (Qwen/JARVIS), the current-vs-planned split, and false-premise corrections."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("nexus_knowledge", "n21b")
d = B.d
K1 = "You are the NEXUS assistant. Describe NEXUS accurately: separate what is implemented today from what is only planned."
K2 = "You are an assistant that knows the NEXUS project. Be precise about what exists now and what is future work. Do not invent features."
K3 = "You are a helpful assistant with knowledge of the NEXUS platform. Say clearly when something is planned rather than built."

# ---- Qwen role ----
d("n21_qwen_plan", "So is Qwen part of NEXUS or not?", "Not yet. Qwen3-8B is the planned generative model for a JARVIS-style assistant, but nothing has been trained, downloaded or integrated, and nothing in the NEXUS application refers to it today.", sys=K1)
d("n21_qwen_plan", "What will Qwen actually do for NEXUS once it exists?", "The plan is for it to be the brain: converse, reason, explain, help with code, and decide whether to answer directly, ask a clarifying question or request a tool. NEXUS would supply the data and services, tools would supply controlled capabilities, and voice would be the interface. All of that is a plan, not something built.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Why Qwen3-8B and not a bigger model?", "The project aims for open weights, free training on Kaggle and local inference with no paid APIs. An 8B model is about the largest that QLoRA can fine-tune on a free 16 GB GPU, though it is tight. The choice is a planning decision and still has to be proven by evaluation.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Will Qwen be the only model in NEXUS?", "The roadmap calls it the single generative model. DeBERTa is a classifier and not a generative model, so keeping it would not count as a second language model, and whether it stays as a cheap router is to be decided after evaluation.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "When will Qwen be in NEXUS?", "There is no date. The roadmap has eight phases, and integration is phase 7, after training and an evaluation gate. Right now only the specification and dataset work have happened, and nothing has been trained.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Explain JARVIS in the context of this project.", "JARVIS is the name for the envisioned assistant experience: a model that converses, reasons, plans and, through tools, acts on your behalf in NEXUS, with voice as the interface. It's a vision for the later phases. Today NEXUS has a voice page that routes requests but cannot answer them.", sys=K2)
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Is JARVIS something I can use now?", "No. It's the long-term goal. What you can use today is the classifier-based /assistant page, which tells you where a request belongs, and the propose and confirm flow for some actions.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "What's the difference between Qwen and DeBERTa here?", "DeBERTa is a small encoder fine-tuned as a classifier: sentence in, one of 14 labels out. It is in the application today. Qwen3-8B is a large generative model that would produce text and decide on tool use. It is only planned.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Does 'brain, body, tools, voice' mean they're all separate programs?", "Conceptually they are separate roles. Qwen is the brain that decides, NEXUS is the body holding data and services, tools are capabilities the brain may request, and voice is the interface. In the planned design the model would run as its own local process, with the backend talking to it through a thin client.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Who is allowed to touch my data in the Qwen design?", "Per the plan, the model would never touch data directly. It could only request tools, the runtime would inject the user id instead of trusting model output, writes would need confirmation, and permissions would be set per tool. None of this exists yet.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "why not let qwen run inside the FastAPI backend", "The plan avoids that. The backend is pinned to a CPU build of torch and transformers for the classifier, and an 8B model is too heavy for that environment. A separate local inference process keeps the pinned stack untouched.")
d("n21_qwen_plan", "Does NEXUS already have a place in the code where Qwen plugs in?", "Not as built code. The architecture plan names integration points such as a model service, a chat endpoint, a conversation manager and a tool layer, but each is marked as missing today. The existing ML package only shows the pattern to follow, which is load once and answer 503 when unavailable.")

# ---- training plan ----
d("n21_training", "Where will you train Qwen, and why not on my laptop?", "The plan is QLoRA fine-tuning on a free Kaggle GPU. Even with 4-bit quantisation, an 8B model needs roughly 10 to 14 GB of GPU memory for training, while the local RTX 3050 laptop GPU reports about 4 GB. So the laptop is for dataset work, validation and tiny dry runs only.")
d("n21_training", "Has training started?", "No. No training has been run, no Kaggle GPU has been used and Qwen3-8B has not been downloaded. So far there has been planning and dataset preparation.")
d("n21_training", "What is QLoRA in simple terms?", "A way to fine-tune a large model cheaply. The base weights are loaded in 4-bit form and frozen, and only small added adapter matrices are trained. The result is an adapter that sits on top of the base model. That is the planned method for Qwen3-8B.")
d("n21_training", "How many examples does the training plan suggest for a first run?", "The training plan suggests roughly 4 to 8 thousand examples for a first run, with a mix of general replay data, assistant behaviour, NEXUS knowledge, tool use and boundary cases. The Phase 2 dataset is larger than that and will be mixed and tuned later.")
d("n21_training", "Why does the plan mix in general data?", "To preserve general ability. Fine-tuning only on assistant and NEXUS behaviour can make a model forget broad skills, so the plan includes replay data and a retention check against the base model.")
d("n21_training", "Does the dataset teach Qwen to browse the web?", "No. The dataset teaches judgement: when a tool would be needed, how to ask for it, and how to say plainly that a capability is unavailable. Browsing would need a real web tool in the runtime, and none exists.", sys=K3)
d("n21_training", "If Qwen is trained on NEXUS facts, does it become connected to NEXUS?", "No. Training teaches what NEXUS is and how to behave. Being connected to NEXUS data or services depends on a runtime tool layer, which is planned but not built.")
d("n21_training", "Why keep the Qwen workspace separate from the app?", "Isolation. Data, scripts, checkpoints and reports live in their own directories, with their own environment, so nothing in NEXUS (code, dependencies, database, configuration or the DeBERTa artifacts) is modified until an integration phase is approved.")
d("n21_training", "Will the fine-tuned model be checked before it goes live?", "That is the evaluation gate. The plan compares it with base Qwen3-8B on reasoning, NEXUS knowledge, tool selection, argument correctness, clarification, failure recovery and hallucinated execution, and also checks that general ability is retained. If it fails, integration does not happen.")
d("n21_training", "Is training loss enough to say the model is ready?", "No. The plan says loss alone is not accepted. Behaviour on held-out, differently written test sets matters, especially because template-generated data once produced 97% in-distribution and 75% on real phrasing in the DeBERTa project.")
d("n21_training", "What chat format will the data end up in?", "That is still to be settled. The training plan describes a Qwen chat template with a tool-call format, and a decision on thinking versus non-thinking mode that must be identical in training and serving. The tool manifest comes in a later phase.")

# ---- roadmap ----
d("n21_roadmap", "List the eight roadmap phases.", "1. Agent specification and architecture\n2. Dataset engineering\n3. Tool manifest and simulation\n4. Training pipeline preparation, with a dry run\n5. Qwen3-8B training\n6. Evaluation against the base model\n7. Runtime and NEXUS integration\n8. The JARVIS and Command Center experience\n\nEach phase has a gate, and integration cannot start until evaluation passes.")
d("n21_roadmap", "Where are we in the roadmap?", "Around the dataset stage. The specification and audit are done, and the training dataset is being built and refined. The tool manifest, training preparation, training, evaluation and integration are all still ahead.")
d("n21_roadmap", "Why is the tool manifest a separate phase after the dataset?", "Because the dataset's tool-use examples should match a real, agreed tool schema. Tool schemas are to be defined once, and every tool call in the data validated against them, with tools simulated during training.")
d("n21_roadmap", "What is checked in the training-preparation phase?", "The planned work is a dry run of the pipeline, tokenizer and chat-template checks, and measurements such as how fast a quantised 8B model would run on this laptop, rather than assuming it.")
d("n21_roadmap", "What happens in phase 7?", "Planned runtime and NEXUS integration: a separate inference process, a chat endpoint, a conversation store decision, a tool registry and executor, a permission layer and the frontend work. It only starts if the evaluation gate passes.")
d("n21_roadmap", "Can the project skip evaluation if the model seems fine in chat?", "Not according to the plan. Integration is gated on a pass report, and informal impressions are explicitly not enough.")

# ---- tool layer plan ----
d("n21_toolplan", "How would Qwen create a task in NEXUS?", "In the plan, it would request a task-creating tool. NEXUS tools would call the same owner-scoped services the app uses now, writes would follow propose and confirm semantics, and the runtime would supply the user id. This is a design, not a working feature.")
d("n21_toolplan", "What stops the model from inventing tool results?", "Training will include contrast examples, but the plan is explicit that training alone is not a guarantee. The runtime is supposed to enforce it: tool results come only from the executor, the model can't write tool turns, and every claim of action must match a recorded successful call.")
d("n21_toolplan", "Will file and terminal tools exist?", "They are part of the future plan, each as a separate gate that is off by default. Paths would be allowlisted, modelled on the developer module's allowlist, and terminal commands would need an allowlist and confirmation. Today none of these tools exist.")
d("n21_toolplan", "How are step limits handled in the plan?", "The runtime would impose a hard step limit and a timeout per request, validate tool arguments against a schema before executing and write entries to the audit log. Those are design rules to be enforced in code and not trusted to training.")
d("n21_toolplan", "Which tool permissions are planned?", "Each tool would have a policy of automatic, confirm or deny. Read-only tools might run automatically, writes and destructive actions would require confirmation, and risky tools could be denied outright.")
d("n21_toolplan", "Would the model see other users' data?", "The plan says no. All NEXUS tools would be scoped to the owner, and the runtime injects the owner id instead of reading it from anything the model outputs.")
d("n21_toolplan", "Why is the web tool considered risky?", "Because it widens the attack surface: fetched pages can contain hostile instructions, and downloads and size limits matter. The plan calls for search and fetch with size and domain limits as a separate, deliberately enabled gate. It does not exist today.")
d("n21_toolplan", "Will Command Center show what the AI did?", "That is the plan: panels for tool traces and pending confirmations. Today the Command Center shows priorities, recommendations, activity and quick actions, with no tool traces because there is no tool layer.")

# ---- serving / hardware ----
d("n21_hardware", "Will Qwen run on my laptop?", "Maybe, slowly. A 4-bit 8B model needs roughly 5 to 6 GB, more than the 4 GB GPU, so part would run on the CPU, and the machine has 15.6 GB of RAM. Expect a few tokens per second. The plan says to measure it before deciding.")
d("n21_hardware", "Is that fast enough for voice?", "Possibly not. Voice needs low latency, and a few tokens per second from a partly CPU-offloaded model may feel slow. This is one of the reasons the plan defers the decision to measurements, with free options such as a smaller quantisation or serving from a Kaggle or Colab session for demos.")
d("n21_hardware", "What hardware does this machine have?", "According to the audit: Windows 11, Python 3.13, Node 24, an RTX 3050 Laptop GPU reporting about 4 GB, an Intel Iris Xe, 15.6 GB of RAM and plenty of free disk space on E:. That's enough for dataset work, not for training an 8B model.")
d("n21_hardware", "Why is Kaggle's T4 'tight' for this?", "A T4 has 16 GB and QLoRA training of Qwen3-8B is estimated at 10 to 14 GB with gradient checkpointing, so there is little headroom. The T4 also lacks bf16, and sessions have time and weekly quota caps. These limits must be re-verified before the real run.")
d("n21_hardware", "Would Qwen replace the GPU model of DeBERTa?", "They are unrelated in memory terms. The classifier runs on a pinned CPU build and is small. Qwen would run in a separate process, so the two don't compete inside the backend.")

# ---- DeBERTa future / voice future ----
d("n21_future", "What's going to happen to the DeBERTa router?", "Undecided. The plan says not to delete or replace it during Qwen training. After evaluation it could remain as a fast pre-router or guard, or it could be retired.")
d("n21_future", "How will the /assistant page change?", "The voice hook is planned to send the transcript to a chat endpoint and speak the reply instead of calling the routing endpoint. The copy and tests that currently say 'NEXUS routes, it does not answer' would need deliberate updates. Speech recognition would stay browser-based unless a separate decision changes it.")
d("n21_future", "Will conversation history be stored on the server later?", "Undecided. The plan lists it as a decision: either server-side history with new database tables, or history held by the client. The current voice design deliberately never persists transcripts, so any change needs an explicit privacy decision.")
d("n21_future", "What project rules will have to change for Qwen?", "The 'NEXUS runs no language model' stance is written into the requirements header, the Phase 12 voice document, a test about offline and local-first behaviour and the README's Ollama claim. The plan says to update those deliberately in the same change as integration, never silently.")
d("n21_future", "Is it a problem that the README mentions Ollama?", "It is a documentation inaccuracy more than a code problem, because no Ollama code exists. When Qwen is eventually served, the docs should be corrected to describe what is actually used.")

# ---- current vs planned contrast ----
d("n21_contrast", "Quick table: what exists today and what is only planned?", "Exists today:\n- Intent classifier (DeBERTa, 14 intents)\n- Propose and confirm actions\n- Browser voice (Web Speech API in, speechSynthesis out)\n- The /assistant routing page\n- Global search, command palette, Command Center\n\nPlanned, not built:\n- Qwen3-8B as a generative model\n- A chat endpoint and chat UI\n- A tool registry and executor\n- File, terminal and web tools\n- A server-side conversation store", sys=K1)
d("n21_contrast", "Which of these are real: chat UI, voice input, tool calling, intent routing?", "Voice input and intent routing are real; the browser does the speech recognition and the DeBERTa classifier does the routing. A chat UI and tool calling are not built. They are part of the plan.")
d("n21_contrast", "Is the propose-confirm flow the same as tool calling?", "No. It is a closed set of action kinds built by hand-written parsers, with you confirming each proposal. Tool calling in the plan means a model requesting tools from a schema-validated registry. The first exists, the second does not.")
d("n21_contrast", "Compare the classifier with the planned Qwen assistant.", "The classifier is built, small, runs on CPU, outputs one of 14 labels and handles one sentence at a time. The Qwen assistant is only planned, would be a large generative model, would produce text, hold a conversation and decide about tools. The classifier's destinations are deterministic services, so Qwen would not be a drop-in replacement for the router.")
d("n21_contrast", "What's built, in-progress and not started for the AI side?", "Built: the DeBERTa classifier, routing and the confirm-gated actions. In progress: specification and dataset preparation for the Qwen assistant. Not started: tool manifest, training, evaluation, any serving or integration.")
d("n21_contrast", "Which parts of the JARVIS vision already work?", "Only fragments: a voice interface that routes requests, and a confirmation-gated action flow. The conversational, reasoning and tool-using parts depend on the planned model and runtime and do not exist yet.", sys=K2)
d("n21_contrast", "Is NEXUS 'agentic' today?", "No. Nothing plans, loops or calls tools on its own. A classifier labels a sentence, a hand-written proposal layer prepares a bounded action, and you confirm. The agent behaviour is a goal for the planned assistant.")
d("n21_contrast", "What's the one sentence summary of NEXUS AI today versus tomorrow?", "Today it is a DeBERTa intent router with confirm-gated actions and browser voice; the planned future is a fine-tuned Qwen3-8B that converses and requests tools, which has not been built.")

# ---- false premises ----
d("n21_false", "Can you show me the Qwen settings page in NEXUS?", "There isn't one, because nothing in NEXUS refers to Qwen yet. The ML settings that exist are for the DeBERTa classifier. Qwen is a planned model and has not been downloaded or integrated.")
d("n21_false", "Since Ollama handles the chat, how do I pull a different model?", "There is no chat and no Ollama in the application, so there is nothing to pull into. The README mentions Ollama, but the backend has no Ollama code. A local language model is part of the plan, and its serving runtime has not been chosen.")
d("n21_false", "My NEXUS assistant gave a wrong answer yesterday. How do I report it?", "The /assistant page doesn't generate answers, so it can't have produced a wrong answer in that sense. It shows a routing decision. If the routing was wrong, that is a classifier miss, and they do happen: the model scored 75% on hand-written requests. If you saw an actual reply, something else was involved, and I'd want to know what you saw.")
d("n21_false", "Why is NEXUS's GPT slow today?", "NEXUS has no GPT or other language model. If something was slow, it was probably the classifier or the backend. The classifier runs on a CPU build, which is fine for a small model but can be slower on a busy machine.")
d("n21_false", "NEXUS's AI remembered my preferences from last month, impressive!", "I'd be careful with that assumption. The assistant keeps at most 20 turns in the browser without persistence, and the classifier sees one sentence at a time, so it doesn't learn preferences. If something looked remembered, it came from stored data in the app, such as your records.")
d("n21_false", "How accurate is NEXUS at summarising my meetings?", "NEXUS doesn't summarise meetings. It has no generative model, so there is no summarising feature to measure. The only model is the intent classifier.")
d("n21_false", "Which cloud provider hosts the NEXUS AI?", "None. The classifier runs locally in the backend process from a checkpoint, and the project's rules excluded cloud AI APIs. The one external dependency for AI-related features is the browser vendor's speech recognition service in Chrome and Edge.")
d("n21_false", "Great, since Qwen is trained, can we skip the dataset work?", "Qwen3-8B hasn't been trained for this project, so the dataset work is the next step, not something to skip. No training has run and nothing has been downloaded.")
d("n21_false", "I heard NEXUS can control my computer by voice.", "That's not accurate. Voice input is transcribed by the browser and only text reaches the backend, which classifies it. NEXUS has no tools for controlling your computer, and the only actions it can take are confirmed changes to its own records.")
d("n21_false", "NEXUS uses my microphone data to train its model, right?", "No. NEXUS doesn't train on voice. The browser transcribes your speech, the backend receives text only, and the classifier was trained earlier from datasets in the ML package. Note that Chrome and Edge do send audio to their own vendors for transcription.")
d("n21_false", "Given NEXUS is built on Ollama, what is the default port?", "NEXUS isn't built on Ollama, so there's no default port to give. The README mentions Ollama, but the code has no Ollama integration.")
d("n21_false", "Does the NEXUS AI write the risk recommendations?", "No. Recommendations come from a detection engine and rules over your recorded data, with evidence attached. No language model generates them.")
d("n21_false", "Can Qwen see my screen in NEXUS?", "No. Qwen is only planned, and there is no screen-viewing capability in the plan or the code. Any future model would see only what a tool or the conversation provides.")
d("n21_false", "Isn't DeBERTa a chatbot model?", "No. DeBERTa is an encoder model. In NEXUS it was fine-tuned for classification, so it outputs a label with a confidence. It can't generate replies.")
d("n21_false", "So the GPU on my laptop is used to run NEXUS's model?", "Not required. The backend runs a pinned CPU build of PyTorch and the classifier is small. The laptop GPU is relevant to the planned Qwen work, where it is too small for training and marginal for inference.")

# ---- scenarios / support ----
d("n21_scenario", "I typed 'summarise my notes from this week' on the assistant page and it said 'Routing to Knowledge'. Why didn't it summarise?", "Because the page routes rather than answers. The classifier decided the request belongs to the knowledge area, and the spoken line is a routing statement. Summarising needs a generative model, which NEXUS doesn't have yet. You can use the knowledge area and search to find the notes yourself.")
d("n21_scenario", "The assistant said 'uncertain' for my request. Did it break?", "No, that's working as designed. Below the 0.90 confidence threshold, the classifier reports the request as uncertain and lists the runner-up intents instead of routing it. Rephrasing with more specific words often helps.")
d("n21_scenario", "I asked it to help me debug a Python error and got generation_unavailable.", "That's the expected response today. The classifier recognised a code_assist request, but NEXUS has no language model to hand it to. It's a stated capability gap and not a bug, and a fine-tuned Qwen model is planned to fill it.")
d("n21_scenario", "The ML status shows unavailable and my dashboards still load. Is that normal?", "Yes. If the checkpoint is missing or ML is disabled, the ML endpoints answer 503 ml_unavailable, while the rest of the app keeps working. Check ML_ENABLED and ML_MODEL_PATH if you expect the classifier to be loaded.")
d("n21_scenario", "I said 'schedule a meeting with Dana tomorrow' and it proposed something odd. What now?", "Don't confirm it. Proposals are only executed after you confirm, so you can discard it. The slot extraction uses hand-written parsers and handles a closed set of action kinds, so unusual phrasing can produce a poor proposal. Rewording or using the planner directly is safer.")
d("n21_scenario", "My teammate says we should wire the classifier into email. Possible?", "NEXUS has no email capability today, and the confirmed actions only change its own records, such as tasks and projects. Email would be a new tool with its own permission and security design, so it would belong with the planned tool layer and not a quick change.")
d("n21_scenario", "We want the assistant to answer questions about our project notes. Where do we start?", "That needs a generative model, which doesn't exist in NEXUS yet. The roadmap's route is a fine-tuned Qwen3-8B behind a separate inference process, with a tool that searches your notes through the existing search layer. In the meantime, global search and the knowledge area are what you can use.")
d("n21_scenario", "Can I rely on the assistant page for accessibility?", "Partly. It offers typed input as well as voice, so users without speech recognition aren't blocked. Voice input depends on browser support, which Firefox lacks, and the spoken replies are routing statements and not answers.")
d("n21_scenario", "Our security team asks what leaves the device when I use voice.", "In Chrome and Edge the audio is sent to the browser vendor for transcription. The NEXUS backend receives only the transcribed text at POST /api/v1/ml/route, and text-to-speech is local. The conversation log is stored only in the browser and is not persisted.")
d("n21_scenario", "Product asks: can we promise customers an AI chat next quarter?", "I wouldn't promise it on the basis of today's state. A chat feature needs a trained and evaluated model, an inference service, a chat endpoint, permissions and UI, and none of those exist. The roadmap gates integration on an evaluation pass, so there's no date yet.", sys=K2)
d("n21_scenario", "A reviewer says the README claims Ollama. Do we correct it?", "That seems right. The audit found no Ollama code, and the requirements file says Ollama is deliberately absent. Editing the README is outside the isolated Qwen workspace, so it would need your approval as a separate change.")
d("n21_scenario", "What would you test first after Qwen is integrated?", "Based on the evaluation plan: whether it claims actions it didn't perform, whether it picks the right tool or admits that none is available, whether it asks for clarification when needed, and whether it still reasons well in general. Also whether the existing 'NEXUS routes, not answers' copy and tests were updated deliberately.")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    save("nexus21_b_test", B.records())
